# Line-ending policy for byte-significant files.
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# Several suites hash checked-in artifacts byte-for-byte -- compatibility baselines, golden
# documents, schema fixtures, and evidence attachments. Git stores them with LF, but on a Windows
# checkout with core.autocrlf=true they land in the worktree as CRLF. The bytes a test hashes are
# therefore platform-dependent, while the expected hash beside it is not.
#
# The symptom is that a suite passes on CI and fails on a Windows machine, with a hash mismatch that
# says nothing about line endings and points at no real defect. That cost real review time on
# PR #1330: an architect measured the architecture suite green where a Windows checkout measured it
# red, and the difference was entirely this.
#
# `eol=lf` pins the worktree representation to the index representation on every platform, so a
# hashed artifact is the same bytes everywhere. These files stay `text`, so they still diff and merge
# normally -- this is not a binary declaration.
#
# `GroundworkArtifactLineEndingGuardTests` fails if one of these files reaches a worktree with CRLF,
# so a newly added hashed artifact that this file does not cover is reported rather than discovered
# later as a mystery hash mismatch.

# Compatibility baselines and approved-difference registries.
tests/**/Baselines/**       text eol=lf

# Golden documents regenerated by ELSA_UPDATE_GOLDENS.
tests/**/Goldens/**         text eol=lf

# Versioned schema fixtures -- the document-version bridge hashes these.
tests/**/Fixtures/**        text eol=lf

# Coverage ledgers and their immutable evidence attachments.
specs/**/coverage-ledger.json       text eol=lf
specs/**/ledger-attachments/**      text eol=lf
specs/**/versions/**                text eol=lf
specs/**/evidence/**                text eol=lf

# Checked-in performance and migration evidence.
docs/reports/**/*.json      text eol=lf

# Binary artifacts that live under the paths above. These must never be line-ending converted --
# a text declaration would rewrite bytes inside a compressed stream and silently corrupt the fixture.
# Listed after the rules above so they win.
*.gz        binary
*.zip       binary
*.nupkg     binary
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.dll       binary
*.db        binary
